    <DataNode elementId="AT5G18100-MONOMER" textLabel="Superoxide dismutase [Cu-Zn] 3" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="AT5G18100.1" dataSource="TAIR gene name" />
      <Graphics centerX="450" centerY="335" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="pmngroup">NIL The cDNA encoding the copper/zinc -dependent superoxide dismutase (SOD) CSD3 has been isolated from &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis thaliana&lt;/i&gt;. CSD3 belongs to a small gene family in Arabidopsis with at least three copper/zinc-dependent SOD&apos;s. The CSD3 gene was expressed in rosette leaves under standard 16-h-photoperiod growth conditions but only approximately 5% compared to CSD2 |CITS: [9765550]|. CSD3 was induced by high-light pulse, ozone fumigation and UV-B irradiation, indicating the role of this peroxisomal SOD to detoxify reactive oxygen species (ROS) that may be generated in this compartment. It has been demonstrated that a copper-chaperone (AtCCS, At1g12520) is crucial for the activation of all three Cu/Zn-dependent SOD&apos;s in Arabdiopsis |CITS: [16126858]|.</Comment>
